The direct collision family fails H: on planted random coefficients, a random direction has an enlarged derivative kernel about 50% of the time, and Gaussian elimination finds a witness immediately. I’m discarding it.

The paper’s computational section offers a better family: recover a CCZ-style affine equivalence between two scrambled graph point sets derived from a Dillon hexanomial. I can sample the full affine witness uniformly first, so there is no planted block structure; verification is just exact set substitution. This is an affine point-set/code-equivalence search problem, with no known polynomial-time recovery method in general.
Full-paper review rejected planted non-APN collisions as easy under a random-direction kernel attack. Proceeding with the paper's CCZ-equivalence classification task, represented as affine equivalence of two independently scrambled graph point sets.
